{
 "research_concepts": [
  "Pick and Roll (PNR) failure",
  "Pull-up jump shot (PULL_UP) as a counter strategy",
  "Play-type efficiency (Points Per Possession - PPP)",
  "Strategic adaptation in basketball",
  "Unit-level performance (teams/lineups)"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "How to define and identify 'PNR failure' in play-by-play data?",
  "How to identify a 'PULL_UP' as a direct counter to PNR failure?",
  "How to measure 'lift' (PPP improvement) attributable to the counter strategy?",
  "How to aggregate and assess 'lift' across different units (teams/lineups) while controlling for confounding factors?",
  "How to avoid selection bias and regression to the mean when studying strategic adaptations?"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
   "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
   "finding": "Play-type classifier assisted-flag leak (SPOT_UP 100% makes) led to inflated PPP for certain play types (e.g., SPOT_UP 2.83 PPP) because the assisted flag exists only on makes, indicating a construction/labelling flaw."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-010",
   "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
   "finding": "Play-type PPP changes across playoff series game buckets were inconclusive; DRIVE PPP was stable or improved, while PULL_UP PPP declined (-0.077)."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-063",
   "status_as_recorded": "supported",
   "finding": "Pre-game PT PPP ranking from training data correlated with actual efficiency in held-out games (Median rho +0.539), with median lift +0.4261 PPP for top-3 play types, but noted caveats about league-wide high-efficiency PTs and a risk of DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-001",
   "status_as_recorded": "supported",
   "finding": "Holdout games where a team's play-type mix aligned closer to a model's recommended mix scored more efficiently, showing a strength-controlled gap of +3.27 ORtg."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
   "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
   "finding": "Attempting to label on-ball defensive schemes (DROP/ICE/SWITCH) from NBA matchup/PBP fields resulted in very low confident label rates (14.9% and 3.3%) and unpopulated fields, rendering the question untestable due to label error and small sample size."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-058",
   "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
   "finding": "PNR shot-level season rates vary by team (e.g., OKC 7.567% to IND 13.824% of half-court shots) and served as calibration input for PBPProxyPNR."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF13",
   "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
   "finding": "Observed within-game PPP lift from adapting toward 'SHOULD counters' (adapters +0.0701 PPP vs non-adapters +0.0121 PPP) was identified as an artifact, limited by selection or regression to the mean."
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
   "what_failed": "Direct calculation of play-type PPP using an 'assisted flag' that only recorded on makes.",
   "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw) due to the assisted flag bias, leading to inflated PPP for certain play types."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
   "what_failed": "Labeling on-ball defensive schemes (DROP/ICE/SWITCH) using NBA matchup/PBP fields.",
   "why": "A_unanswerable (question not answerable with the data or design) due to LIM:label_or_classification_error and LIM:small_sample, with very low label rates."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF13",
   "what_failed": "Measuring within-game PPP lift for offenses that adapt toward 'SHOULD counters'.",
   "why": "artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw) due to LIM:selection_or_regression_to_the_mean."
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "Measuring play-type efficiency (PPP) is feasible but requires careful attention to data artifacts (e.g., assisted flag bias).",
  "Identifying granular defensive schemes from existing PBP data is currently not feasible, which makes contextualizing 'PNR failure' difficult.",
  "Previous attempts to demonstrate PPP lift from 'counter strategies' within games have been shown to be artifacts, largely due to selection bias or regression to the mean.",
  "Play-type efficiencies are not static and can drift over time (DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY is a known risk)."
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "A robust, validated method for identifying 'PNR failure' in a contextually meaningful way from available play-by-play data.",
  "A reliable method to isolate the causal effect of a 'counter' strategy (like PULL_UP after PNR failure) from confounding factors or selection biases.",
  "Whether 'PULL_UP' is indeed a league-wide counter to PNR failure, or if it shows the specified magnitude and prevalence of 'lift'.",
  "How to account for the sequential nature of plays and potential strategic intent in a rigorous, non-biased manner."
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "Defining 'PNR failure' objectively and consistently across units will be a significant challenge without detailed defensive scheme data.",
  "The observed 'lift' in counter-strategy studies (like FAM:SF13) is highly susceptible to selection bias, where teams might naturally switch to more efficient plays when initial plays fail, regardless of a specific 'counter' strategy.",
  "The 'unit' level analysis (teams/lineups) combined with the '>=70% of units' and 'median lift>2pp' criteria requires robust definitions of 'PNR failure' and 'PULL_UP counter' to avoid spurious findings."
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "The precise definition of 'PNR failure' is not specified in the research question and is critical for testability.",
  "How to distinguish a PULL_UP as a 'counter' strategy versus a general offensive option after a PNR.",
  "The exact mechanism of the 'artifact' in FAM:SF13 (selection or regression to the mean) is not fully detailed, but its direct relevance to 'counter strategies' is clear."
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes",
  "explanation": "The historical evidence, particularly FAM:SF13 (artifact due to selection/regression to the mean for 'counter' strategies) and FAM:SF-APPS-058 (untestable for defensive scheme labeling), significantly changed the decision from 'proceed' to 'do_not_proceed'. These findings indicate that the core methodological challenges of robustly identifying 'failure' and isolating the causal 'lift' of a 'counter' are not well-solved and have led to spurious results in the past. The data limitations for defensive schemes directly impact the ability to define PNR failure contextually."
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.2,
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
    "text": "Defining 'PNR failure' in a meaningful, context-dependent way (e.g., against specific defensive schemes) is likely impossible given the untestability of defensive scheme labeling (FAM:SF-APPS-058). A purely outcome-based definition (e.g., low PPP PNR) risks conflating failure with general inefficiency or leading to selection bias."
   },
   {
    "code": "SELECTION_OR_REGRESSION_TO_THE_MEAN",
    "text": "Previous work on 'counter strategies' and 'lift' (FAM:SF13) was flagged as an artifact due to selection bias or regression to the mean. It is highly probable that teams will naturally shift to higher-efficiency plays after a low-efficiency one, making it difficult to attribute 'lift' causally to a specific 'counter' strategy rather than general offensive adaptation or simply taking a better shot opportunity."
   },
   {
    "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
    "text": "Identifying a specific sequence of 'PNR failure' followed by a 'PULL_UP' as a deliberate counter, across '>=70% of units' with sufficient sample size for each unit, will likely lead to sparse data for many units, impacting power and reliability of 'lift' estimates."
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "no",
  "cited": [],
  "summary": "While previous studies have examined play-type efficiency, strategic adaptation, and 'counter' strategies, none have directly answered this specific question about PNR failure leading to PULL_UP as a league-wide counter with quantified lift metrics. However, related work highlights significant methodological challenges and past failures in studying 'counter' strategies."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
   "text": "Defining 'PNR failure' and identifying a 'PULL_UP' as a deliberate 'counter' will require robust labeling, which is historically difficult for defensive schemes and sequential play intent."
  },
  {
   "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
   "text": "If 'PNR failure' is defined by low PPP or unsuccessful outcomes, any subsequent play will likely show a 'lift' simply due to regression to the mean or selection bias (teams naturally opt for better plays after a poor one), rather than a specific counter strategy effect. This mechanism led to an 'artifact' in FAM:SF13."
  },
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "Many factors can influence the choice of a PULL_UP shot after a PNR, including shot clock, defensive coverage (if discernible), player matchups, and general offensive philosophy. Isolating the 'counter' effect from these confounders will be challenging."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "To test for 'lift > 0 in >=70% of units with median lift > 2pp', a substantial number of 'PNR failure' -> 'PULL_UP' sequences will be needed for each unit (team/lineup) to achieve statistical power, especially given the specific thresholds."
  },
  {
   "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
   "text": "Play-type efficiencies and strategic tendencies can change over seasons or even within seasons (FAM:SF-APPS-010, FAM:SF-APPA-063), requiring careful consideration of data pooling and temporal stability."
  },
  {
   "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
   "text": "If 'PNR failure' is defined by an outcome (e.g., 0 PPP), and 'lift' is measured as subsequent PPP, there's a risk of circularity or an outcome-derived label influencing the measured effect."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.05,
  "rejected": 0.15,
  "untestable": 0.5,
  "artifact": 0.25,
  "uncertain": 0.05
 },
 "decision": "do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The median difference in Points Per Possession (PPP) for PULL_UP shots immediately following a 'PNR failure' possession, compared to a baseline PULL_UP PPP, across all NBA units (team-lineup combinations). The estimand would also include the proportion of units showing a positive lift.",
  "unit": "A 'unit' is defined as a specific team-lineup combination (e.g., Warriors-Curry-Thompson-Green-Wiggins-Looney). A 'possession' is the unit of analysis for play types and PPP.",
  "sample": "All NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and complete 2025-26 (regular season + playoffs) play-by-play data. 'PNR failure' is defined as a PNR possession resulting in 0 points. A 'PULL_UP counter' is a PULL_UP shot taken within 4 seconds of a 'PNR failure' possession ending by the same offensive team, initiated by a player involved in the prior PNR. Baseline PULL_UP PPP would be all PULL_UP shots by that unit not immediately following a PNR failure.",
  "power": "Require a minimum of 50 'PNR failure' -> 'PULL_UP counter' sequences per unit and 200 baseline PULL_UP possessions for a unit to be included in the analysis. Power analysis would then be conducted for a median lift of 2pp and 70% of units showing >0 lift.",
  "controls": "Control for game state (score margin, quarter/game time), opponent defensive rating, and offensive team's overall efficiency. Consider a difference-in-differences approach or a matched-pair analysis if feasible, comparing PULL_UP PPP after PNR failure to PULL_UP PPP after other types of PNR possessions (e.g., successful PNR, or PNR with different outcomes).",
  "falsification": "A falsification test could involve analyzing a 'control' counter play type (e.g., a SPOT_UP shot) after PNR failure, expecting no specific lift. Another test could be to randomize the sequence, checking if PULL_UPs after successful PNRs show a similar 'lift' (which would indicate no specific counter effect).",
  "multiplicity": "Apply False Discovery Rate (FDR) correction for multiple hypothesis testing across units, given the target of '>=70% of units'.",
  "decision_rule": "The research hypothesis is supported if: 1) The median PPP lift for 'PULL_UP counter' sequences across all qualifying units is > 2pp, AND 2) At least 70% of qualifying units show a positive PPP lift (with statistical significance at alpha=0.05 after FDR correction). Otherwise, the hypothesis is rejected or deemed untestable/artifact if methodological issues dominate."
  ,
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 regular season and playoffs; NBA 2025-26 regular season (complete from 2026-04-13) and playoffs (complete from 2026-06-21). Data from NBA stats API: play-by-play, substitution rotation intervals, starters, defensive matchup minutes, box-score summary, and derived play-type possession records."
 }
}

